---
.title = "LLMD quickstart",
.description = "Start LLMD in a CUDA container and send your first chat request with curl.",
.date = .date("2026-11-16T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
---
Serve Liquid AI's LFM3-1.7B FP8 checkpoint on a local port, then confirm that
LLMD can generate a reply through its OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint.

# [Before you start]($heading.id('before-you-start'))

Use a Linux host with an NVIDIA GPU that supports this FP8 checkpoint and has
enough available memory to serve it. Install the NVIDIA driver, Docker, and the
[NVIDIA Container Toolkit](https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/cloud-native/container-toolkit/latest/install-guide.html).
Have `curl` and network access to Docker Hub and Hugging Face. See
[Compatibility]($link.page('reference/compatibility')) for other platforms and
checkpoint formats.

# [1. Start the server]($heading.id('start-the-server'))

```sh
docker pull zmlai/llmd:20261106.0-cuda
docker run --rm --name llmd \
  --gpus=all --shm-size=256GB -p 127.0.0.1:8000:8000 \
  zmlai/llmd:20261106.0-cuda --model=hf://LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8
```

Keep this terminal open. `--gpus=all` exposes the NVIDIA devices to the
container. `--shm-size=256GB` sets the shared-memory limit used in the upstream
example. The host port is bound to loopback. Docker reads arguments before the
image name, and LLMD reads `--model` after it. LLMD loads the checkpoint
directly from [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8).

# [2. Check the loaded model]($heading.id('check-the-loaded-model'))

In a second terminal, query the model list after the startup logs show loading
has finished.

```sh
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/models
```

Use the `id` in `data[0]` as the request's `model`. For this command, the
default model ID is `LFM3-1.7B-FP8`.

# [3. Send a chat request]($heading.id('send-a-chat-request'))

```sh
curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "LFM3-1.7B-FP8",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Reply with one short greeting."}]
  }'
```

Look for `choices[0].message.content` in the JSON response. The generated text
varies. If you already have an LLMD endpoint, use its URL, model ID, and any
authentication headers required by the deployment. To stop the local example,
press Ctrl+C in the server terminal.

# [Troubleshooting]($heading.id('troubleshooting'))

- **Docker cannot access the GPU.** Check that `nvidia-smi` works on the host
  and that Docker is configured for the NVIDIA Container Toolkit.
- **The endpoint refuses connections.** Check the startup output for model
  access, compilation, or memory errors. Retry `/v1/models` once loading
  completes.
- **The request fails after startup.** Compare the request's model ID with the
  one returned by `/v1/models`.

The [release notes](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261106.0/) list LFM3 support and
the [CUDA recipe](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261029.0/#nvidia) uses this
checkpoint. Execution of this guide on a recorded GPU host is pending.

